  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Shower and thunderstorm chances increase for the \nfirst half of next week with the potential for areas of heavy rain \nand some strong thunderstorms.\n\nAs a strong upper-level ridge expands into the Plains early next \nweek, northwesterly flow aloft will occur across much of the East. \nThis ridge gets pushed back some as a potentially strong upper-level \ntrough in eastern Canada sends spokes of energy southeastward across \nthe Great Lakes and Northeast. This will initially allow for the \nupper-level trough to become more carved out across the Northeast by \nlater this weekend. Some guidance maintains a rather vigorous \nshortwave sliding southeastward across the Great Lakes Monday and \nthen across the Northeast during Tuesday. This results in some model \nsolutions closing off the trough across the Northeast Tuesday into \nWednesday. Some other guidance is slower and thus sharpens or closes \noff the trough a day or two later. The upper-level system will drive \nsurface low pressure and a cold front our way Tuesday into \nWednesday. Some convection from upstream may try and get into parts \nof our area later Sunday, however while weakening there may be just \nenough surface high pressure still over our area with dry enough air \nto protect our area. This will change though as we go through next \nweek.\n\nIt looks like we turn convectively active Tuesday into Wednesday. \nWhile there remains some differences among the model guidance on the \ntiming and strength of the features diving southeastward early next \nweek, the setup would tend to favor upstream convective development \ngiven northwesterly flow aloft. This would be enhanced if the trough \naloft closes off. Deeper moisture (dew points into the low 70s) \nadvecting northward ahead of this feature with eventual cooling \naloft and stronger forcing for ascent may result in widespread \nshowers and some thunderstorms. Depending on the evolution of this \nsystem, some areas of heavy rainfall and strong thunderstorms are \npossible. The intensity and coverage of thunderstorms though may \nultimately depend on the prior days convective development and then \nits evolution downstream.",
